EXTENSION "ArM5_Faith_and_Flame_Arelat_ArgentiusOfMercereTheInvitedRedcap" {
    NAME "Ars Magica Fifth Edition, Faith & Flame - Argentius of Mercere, the Invited Redcap"
    VERSION "0.5.0"
    SPEC_VERSION "0.5"
    RELEASE_DATE "2026-10-06"
    DEPENDS_ON "ArM5_Faith_and_Flame_Core_Ext"

    # Argentius of Mercere, the Invited Redcap (Chapter Eight: Arelat, p134): one character, its printed stat lines verbatim fields, its write-up the DESCRIPTION.

    #t32c69cd53577347af8c3ce ^"Argentius of Mercere, the Invited Redcap" DEF {
        EXTENDS #tHoHTL05ExampleCharacterAA ^"Example Character"
        PROPERTIES {
            ^"Role" STRING "Magus"
            ^"Covenant" LIST [#t6db295248a1e5be20cb0ac ^"Coenobium Rhodanien"]
            ^"Age" STRING "49 (Apparent Age: 41)"
            ^"Personality Traits" STRING "Wrathful +3, Gloomy +2, Pious +2"
            ^"Source" STRING "Faith & Flame"
            ^"Page" INTEGER 134
        }
        DESCRIPTION "This former weapon-master of Valnastium is living a second life. Once known as Auriel Blake, he is now known as the Redcap Argentius. Auriel was seemingly slain by the disgraced magus Damocles in a mundane duel, but then restored to life by a miracle produced by the greater relic of St Michael he carries within his sword pommel. Since recovering from his fatal injury, he has been accepted as a Redcap of the Coenobium as part of Alazais’ plans. Still coping with his return from death, he remains distanced from the other covenfolk, who regard him with a mixture of reverence and fear.\n\nHis resurrection appears to have awakened the latent magical powers of his Heroes’ Birthright Major Virtue, and many magi are uncertain whether to treat him as a hedge magician or respect his unorthodox Redcap status. Argentius has developed a remarkable control over swords and other bladed weapons, and has formed an unlikely friendship with his murderer’s former apprentice, Xiphos.\n\nHe now believes, perhaps correctly, that the sword he carries is the mythical Sword of Ares — the weapon reputedly carried by Attila on his rampage into western Europe as “Scourge of God.” To date he has not allowed investigation of his weapon to verify this claim."
    }

}
